# Canonical Linux Metaplex source experiments

Acceptance requires reconstructed Rust builds. This research packet contains no newly accepted contract or dependency. No assembly, target instruction bytes or captured ELF is supplied to the compiler.

The public Metaplex commit is `17e99434af5ead3e989c08f32af33609821d0bc0`. The baseline applies the retained candidate11 Rust patch. All 812 tracked source files, lockfile included, match candidate11. The pinned Linux/amd64 image is `solanafoundation/solana-verifiable-build@sha256:43206149a565ec35da95b1a909f4714f5d5f0d8241c31df24189e517acd18467`, image config `8a1c7e411dda3083f89fc82e9fbcbc57cfffed615853f11c124ef3df1443978f`. The official Linux v1.37 SDK is mounted read-only; its target Rust loader SHA256 is `7194df9c12e893cfa88fff5dd821d02d45910b4d375e13677b4723f45769a384`. Podman uses the existing Rosetta binfmt on an arm64 VM. This emulated Linux recipe reproduced the earlier hosted Linux output exactly.

`baseline/candidate.so` is the actual clean Rust source build, 707448 bytes, SHA256 `3bbaf27ff0c10ff68d8a195a82f39eadee266f324067bd0a66081710be30b4ec`. It is still 1509 padded positions away from the captured target. The first invocation stalled fetching the full registry index through cargo metadata and was stopped; `baseline/build.log` records that incomplete attempt. `resume-sparse.py`, its command and resumed log record the completed build after explicitly selecting sparse registry transport. `initial-driver-reconstructed.py` is labeled reconstructed: it is the current adapter with the later sparse environment addition removed, not a contemporaneously frozen initial driver.

`build.py` is an adapter of `original-build-helper.py`: Docker transport becomes Podman; read-only pinned SDK and a public registry cache are mounted; CPU/memory/job caps are explicit; sparse registry transport is set for metadata as well as compilation; an owned container can remain alive. The original shared build helper is unchanged. The adapter intentionally rejects dependencies other than Metaplex.

`iterate.py` serializes changes with a nonblocking lock, verifies the live image and known baseline, hashes all tracked sources, removes only the old generated deployment ELF, compiles Rust, then reads the reference for comparison. It allows changes only to Utilize and optionally lib.rs. Each case retains the actual candidate, padded allocation image, exact Rust inputs, source map, command, build log and frozen driver. Paths/container IDs in historical records are locators, not proof that a job is still running.

The 26 successful cached trials are summarized in `results.json`. The positive control reproduces hosted candidate12 (`4f039033…`) exactly. Adding the disclosed `core_intrinsics` feature alone reproduces that same control. Guard permutations, boolean match layout, wrapping subtraction, branch hints, and direct ProgramError conversion all fail whole-text and normalized Utilize equality. The best of these new variants differs at 1514 payload positions, worse than the 1509 baseline. Ordinary subtraction after the complete admission guard reproduces candidate11 exactly, showing that replacing checked_sub with direct subtraction does not repair this layout. None is promoted. These negative results exclude only the measured source forms and recipe; they do not prove source recovery impossible or prove a unique historical compiler.

`python3 repro/pump/metaplex-local-linux-research/verify.py` checks retained artifacts, source-map scope, raw payload differences, executable comparison, controls and the rejected normalized Utilize result. This is an offline evidence verification, not another build. SDK archives and public crate caches remain outside Git; baseline patch and upstream commit bind source reconstruction.

Eight additional full-source CFG trials test eager boolean evaluation, checked subtraction before admission, labeled-loop Result exits, separate return statements, reversing the full inner tail arms, and explicit Data cleanup on rejection. None matches. Eager invalid checks reproduce candidate11; separate returns and a positive inner tail reproduce candidate12. The others emit larger/different code. `failed-source-form/partial-move/` records the unsuccessful whole-Metadata drop form: Rust rejects use after the earlier metadata.uses unwrap partially moves the value (E0382). This is a source type failure, not a measured executable mismatch. The repaired Data-field drop forms compile but worsen byte identity. Three upstream source-history checkpoints are retained; their paths are bound to exact Git commit IDs. They retain the original early invalid guard and checked_sub sequence, so no new historical source form was recovered in that search.

After those experiments, `restored-control/` restores all812 baseline source hashes and recompiles. The actual output again equals candidate11 exactly. This validates returning to the control before diagnostic compiler-output capture.

Two final source trials place the unlikely hint directly on the isolated remaining>=requested condition (one full inner tail, one match expression), rather than on the earlier combined admission expression. Both reproduce candidate12 exactly. The localized hint therefore fails for these full handlers as well. The Linux machine dump, in the separate compiler-output packet, is a faithful baseline control and narrows the earlier CFG hypothesis.

The final `restored-after-hints/` control again restores all812 source hashes and recompiles to candidate11 exactly. The isolated source clone is left on the baseline with lib.rs feature changes removed.
